  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist, and the rule is enabled and reporting status `ok`.\n\n**Dashboard: `EFF-2aef34b8 Web logs overview`**\nPanels (time range last 1 year, since the sample data isn't necessarily recent):\n- Total requests (count)\n- Unique visitors (unique count of `clientip`)\n- Average response size (average of `bytes`)\n- Requests over time (area, date histogram on `@timestamp`)\n- Requests by response code (bar, terms on `response.keyword`)\n- Top source countries (bar, terms on `geo.src`)\n\nI didn't check the rendered values in the browser, so the panels' data is unverified.\n\n**Alert rule: `EFF-2aef34b8 5xx spike`**\n- Type: Elasticsearch query (`.es-query`), consumer `stackAlerts`, tagged `EFF-2aef34b8`\n- Runs every 1m on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`\n- Query: `response.keyword` wildcard `5*`, over a 5-minute window\n- Fires when the count is greater than 10\n- Enabled\n\n**Choices I made:**\n- I used `response.keyword` with a wildcard for 5xx. This assumes the sample data stores `response` as a text field with a keyword subfield, which is what the field list suggests but I didn't confirm.\n- The dashboard uses a one-year range rather than the default of 7 days.\n\n**Issue:** The `KIBANA_TEST_API_KEY` variable isn't set in my shell, so I couldn't query Kibana with raw curl. I explored the fields through the MCP tools instead of ES|QL. This didn't block the task.",
